<title>SPAIN: Spain and Italy are difficult to compare - Rato.</title>
<headline>Spain and Italy are difficult to compare - Rato.</headline>
<dateline>MADRID 1997-04-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>Spanish Economy Minister Rodrigo Rato said on Wednesday it was difficult to make comparisons between Spain and Italy and their respective ability to meet European single currency convergence criteria.</p>
<p>Instead, he said he preferred to make comparisons with countries such as France or Portugal.</p>
<p>&quot;Comparisons with Italy are very difficult. Italy is reducing its inflation, but its economic growth is very weak,&quot; Rato said in a parliamentary speech.</p>
<p>&quot;I think we should compare ourselves to other countries. I would rather talk of Portugal or of France,&quot; he added.</p>
<p>Spain's gross domestic product (GDP) grew 2.6 percent year-on-year in the last quarter of 1996, and is expected to grow at 3.0 percent rate in 1997.</p>
<p>Italian GDP rose a modest 0.1 percent year-on-year in the last quarter of 1996, and is expected to reach a growth rate of around 1.3-1.5 percent in 1997.</p>
<p>-- Madrid Newsroom, + 341 585 8340</p>
</text>
